/* =====================================================================
   LAYER 4 — THE DROWNED GALLERIES
   The "between" band: the surreal nadir of the mortal-sewer descent, below
   the old drains. Everything here is FLOODED — mirror-still black water,
   pillared halls, the half-beat-late reflection, the drowned dead. This is
   where "the dirt drains down to feed" the demons (TheCatacomb). Register:
   not poison anymore, not yet infernal — drowned, patient, kept.

   Hub = DeepHub. Enter from L3 (DrownedHall ← TheOlderStair). Holds two named
   demon bargains: the Whisperer (Cistern) and the Understudy (Gallery). The
   way deeper (TheSunkenStair) foreshadows the true hell-deep below but is not
   yet open — provisional: the drowned galleries are the secret bottom route
   into hell, built when L7-9 land. Depths registered in setup.depthOf (= 4).
   ===================================================================== */


/* ---------- ASCENT (to L3) + HUB ---------- */

:: DrownedHall [deep]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 3>>
The stair down is older than the sewer above it — undressed stone, the treads
worn into shallow bowls by feet that weren't yours. It lets you out into a
flooded hall the brickwork upstairs gave no reason to expect: pillared, vaulted,
far too large, the black water standing dead still and throwing back the dark
like a polished floor.

No machines here, no rats. The smell has changed too — under the sewage,
something older and colder, the breath of a place sealed a very long time. Your
reflection in the standing water holds steady when you move and lags a half-beat
behind when you stop, and you decide not to look at it again.

<<if $sealed>>
The stair you came down by is not behind you anymore — only weeping wall where it
was, and the longer you look the less certain you become that it was ever a stair.
To press your hands to that wall and search it in earnest — to go looking for a way
the place has so patiently convinced you never existed — would not be searching. It
would be the moment you stop being someone who is leaving. There would be no coming
back from it; it is, you understand, an ending.
<<elseif $deepTurns gte setup.SEAL_WARN>>
The stair behind you is harder to keep in mind than it should be.
<</if>>

<<link "Wade deeper, toward the pillared dark">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>
<<if $sealed>>
<<link "Search the wall in earnest — stop being someone who is leaving (an ending)">><<goto "EndingResident">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<link "Back up the old stair, into the drains">><<goto "TheOlderStair">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: DeepHub [deep]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 2>>
<<nobr>>
The water shallows onto a kind of plaza — a meeting of ways like the confluence
far above, but wrong in the manner of a face with all the right features in
slightly the wrong places. Lamps burn in wall-niches with a low wet light,
though nothing here should burn, and the flames don't flicker in the moving air.
<</nobr>>

<<if $sealed>>
You turn to find the stair you came down by, and it isn't there — only weeping
wall where it was, and the longer you look the more certain you become that there
was never a stair at all, that you have always been here. The lamps seem a little
warmer for the thought.
<<elseif $deepTurns gte setup.SEAL_WARN>>
You can feel the place settling around you like silt over something dropped in
still water. Every minute here, the way back up gets harder to picture. You
should go *now*, while going is still a thing you want.
<<else>>
You should not stay here long. The place wants you to stay, and the wanting is
patient, and it is the patience that should scare you.
<</if>>

<<link "A drowned gallery, lined with niches">><<goto "TheCatacomb">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a cistern, where a voice is waiting">><<goto "Cistern">><</link>>
<<link "A mirror-still hall, barring the deeper galleries">><<goto "TheReflectingHall">><</link>>
<<link "A flooded colonnade, off the west of the plaza">><<goto "TheColonnade">><</link>>
<<link "A side-sluice, where a relief-vault is said to hold dry stores">><<goto "TheSluiceFork">><</link>>
<<link "A tiled passage off the plaza, warm with steam and the wrong-here smell of clean water">><<goto "TheBathhouse">><</link>>
<<if $sealed>>
To go searching the wall for the way up now would be to find, with your hands flat on the cold wet
stone, that there is no way up and there is no wall-seam and there never was — and the place is so
warm with the having of you that the finding would feel less like despair than like lying down. It
would be the last thing you do down here as yourself.
<<link "Search for the way up in earnest — and let the place keep you (an ending)">><<goto "EndingResident">><</link>>
<<else>>
<<link "Search the wall for the way back up">><<goto "DrownedHall">><</link>>
<</if>>


/* ---------- THE BATHHOUSE — the drowned sewer-sentō + the kappa (owner 2026-06-18) ----------
   A bathhouse run by kappa, where the hospitality IS the drowning. Bested two ways: the BOW
   (folklore — a kappa must bow back, which spills the water-dish that is its strength; gated by
   composure, i.e. corruption < BOW_CORRUPTION_MAX — the deep in you locks simple courtesy) or by
   FORCE (startFight → TheBathhouseCleared). Either way the spilled dish — the only clean water in
   hell — cleanses you (setup.soothe). $kappaBested gates the encounter; $kappaBowed varies the
   cleared prose. */

:: TheBathhouse [deep]
<<roomart "bathhouse">>
<<nobr>>
The steam-warm passage opens into a room that should not be down here, and is: a bathhouse. A real
one — a sewer //sentō//, sunk into the drowned dark and kept, somehow, in working order. Duckboard
underfoot, worn smooth by generations of bare wet feet. A wall of little wooden lockers for the things
a guest takes off at the door. Low stools and hand-buckets at a washing-wall. And the baths: deep
sunken tubs of standing black water, tiled in cracked blue, steaming gently — and the steam carries,
wrongly, impossibly, the clean mineral smell of an actual hot spring, of the upper world, of //being
clean//, so strong after everything the drains have worked into your skin that your knees go soft with
the wanting of it.
<</nobr>>

<<if $kappaBested>>
<<if $kappaBowed>>
The attendant kneels where it folded, bowed empty, the spilled dish a dark wet star on the blue tile,
and it will not look up at you — a kappa that has lost its water keeps the form of courtesy even in
ruin. The other attendants have withdrawn to the corners and will not meet your eye. The baths steam
on, ownerless, and the clean smell is only a smell now, no longer a hook.
<<else>>
The attendant lies where you put it down on the wet tile, its dish cracked and run dry. The other
attendants have withdrawn to the far corners and will not come near you. The baths steam on,
ownerless.
<</if>>

<<if not $bathhouseLooted>>
Behind the attendant's station is its hoard — the magpie keep of a thing that has worked a bathhouse
for an age and held onto what guests left behind, and what it could not bear to part with.
<<linkreplace "Go through the attendant's hoard">><<nobr>>
<<set $bathhouseLooted to true>>
<<set _c to random(9, 16)>><<run setup.gainCoins(_c)>>
<<set $hp to $maxhp>>
A drift of cold coin — <<= _c>> of it, yours now. A few cucumbers, improbably fresh, and you eat one,
because a kappa's cucumbers are the real thing and your body has forgotten the taste of a real thing,
and it is so plainly, absurdly good that something in you almost breaks. And the bowed attendant, when
you crouch by it, does the last courteous thing left to it: a kappa is a bonesetter in the old stories,
a healer when it is beaten and made to be, and it lays its webbed cold hands on your hurts and puts them
right, the way a host tends a guest, having no water left now to drown you with.
//(+<<= _c>> coins, a real cucumber, and your hurts set right — vigor full)//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
You've been through the hoard. The cucumbers are eaten, the coin is yours, and the bowed attendant has
nothing left to give but the shape of a bow.
<</if>>

<<link "Back out to the plaza">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>

<<else>>
And the attendants. They are small — child-sized, hunched — green-grey and webbed and beaked, with a
shallow stone dish set into the crown of each skull, brimful of clear water that catches the lamplight
when they tip their heads. //Kappa.// They move among the tubs with the noiseless practiced courtesy of
staff who have worked one house a very long time, and when they see you one comes at once, and bows —
low, correct, unhurried — and gestures, with every appearance of kindness, at the nearest steaming tub.

You have spent enough of yourself down here to know what bath it means: the long one, the one you don't
come back up from. A kappa attends a guest //into// the water, and draws something out of them under it,
and keeps it. The clean smell is the bait — the cleanest, cruelest bait the deep has used on you yet.
But the law of the house runs both ways, and you remember it: a guest who bows is bowed to, and a kappa,
bowing, spills its dish.

To bow back invokes the law the attendant cannot refuse — it //must// return the bow, and returning it
tips the dish off its crown, and a kappa without its water is undone, all its drowning strength poured
out on the tile. The catch is yours: a bow like that wants a body that still bends like a person's, a
spine the deep hasn't set into something else.
<<linkreplace "Bow to the attendant, low and proper, as a guest should">><<nobr>>
<<if setup.composedEnoughToBow()>>
<<set $kappaBested to true>><<set $kappaBowed to true>><<run setup.soothe(setup.DISH_CLEANSE)>>
You bow — and it is a //real// bow, the body still remembering how, the back still willing — and the
attendant's courtesy is a reflex far older than its hunger: it bows back, lower than you. The dish on
its crown tips, and the clear water in it — real water, sky-water, rain that fell on the upper world
and was carried down here a thimble at a time and hoarded against exactly this — pours out across the
blue tile and over your bare feet, and the attendant folds in on itself with a small wet sound of pure
dismay, all its strength gone out with the water. Where the clean rain runs over you, some of the deep
washes off.
//(the spilled dish-water washes some of the deep off you — corruption eased)//

<<link "Step past the bowed attendant, into the quiet of the baths">><<goto "TheBathhouse">><</link>>
<<else>>
You go to bow — and you cannot. Not properly. The thing the deep has been making of you will not bend
that way; what comes out of your body is a lurch, a crook-backed parody of a bow, and the attendant
reads it instantly for what it is — not a guest but another of the deep's half-made things putting on
airs — and the kindness goes out of its beaked face all at once. Its dish does not spill. It comes for
you across the wet tile with no courtesy left in it at all.
<<link "Then there's nothing for it but the hard way">><<run setup.startFight("kappa", "TheBathhouseCleared")>><<goto "Combat">><</link>>
<</if>>
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>

Or refuse the ritual outright — keep your guard up, your clothes on, and put the attendant down before
it can work you anywhere near the water. A kappa out of its element is a small thing; a kappa in its
own bathhouse, on its own wet tile, with its dish full on its head, is not the small thing it looks.
<<scentof "kappa">>
<<link "Refuse the bath — put the attendant down by force">><<run setup.startFight("kappa", "TheBathhouseCleared")>><<goto "Combat">><</link>>

<<link "Keep your guard up and back out to the plaza, unattended">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheBathhouseCleared [deep]
<<set $kappaBested to true>>
<<run setup.soothe(setup.DISH_CLEANSE)>>
You put the attendant down on the wet tile, and as it falls the dish cracks off its crown and the water
in it — real water, sky-water, rain that fell on the upper world and was hoarded down here a thimble at
a time — runs out across the blue tile and over your hands, and where the clean rain touches you, some
of the deep washes off.
//(the spilled dish-water washes some of the deep off you — corruption eased)//

<<link "Into the quiet of the steaming baths">><<goto "TheBathhouse">><</link>>


/* ---------- GATE ---------- */

:: TheReflectingHall [deep]
The hall beyond the plaza is the largest yet, and the most still: a black sheet
of standing water from wall to distant wall, unbroken, holding the vaulted dark
above it so perfectly that you cannot find the line where stone ends and reflection
begins. The deeper galleries are across it. There is no bridge. There is only the
water, and the thing the water does — which is to show you yourself, crossing,
a half-beat before you move, and waiting for you on the far side with a patience
you do not like.

<<if $causewayFound>>
But you have the font's secret now: the true causeway runs just under the skin of
the water, and the way to read it is to watch not your feet but your *reflection's*,
which step on the stones a moment before you reach them. You cross dry to the
ankle, and the far-side you turns and walks into the dark a half-beat ahead, and is
gone.

<<link "Across the causeway, into the deeper galleries">><<goto "TheTombIsland">><</link>>
<<else>>
You can't find the footing by looking. The font-chamber off the side might hold
the trick of it — or you can simply wade, straight out into the black, and trust
that something in water this old and this still won't take the offering of a warm
body walking willingly in.

<<link "To the font-chamber, to learn the crossing">><<goto "TheStillFont">><</link>>
<<set _tlbl to "Wade straight across the black (" + setup.tollTag("legL", "filth", 18) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "legL" "filth" 18>>
  <<goto "TheTombIsland">>
<</link>>
<</if>>
<<link "A current tugs from a side-channel — investigate">><<goto "TheUndertow">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the plaza of lamps">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>


/* ---------- CACHES / PUZZLE ---------- */

:: TheStillFont [deep]
A side-chamber off the reflecting hall, and at its centre a font — a wide shallow
basin of the same black water, raised on a plinth carved with the worn figures of
people kneeling to look into it. Whoever built this place came here to *read* the
water. The trick declares itself as you lean in: your reflection in the font moves
first. Lift your hand and the reflection has already lifted its own; the water runs
a half-beat ahead of the world, and shows you what your body is about to do.

<<if $causewayFound>>
You have the way of it now — watch the reflection's feet, not your own. The font
has nothing left to teach.
<<else>>
<<linkreplace "Kneel and learn to read the half-beat">>
<<set $causewayFound to true>><<corrupt "torso" "filth" 2>>
You kneel where a thousand drowned penitents knelt, and you learn the thing they
learned: to trust the reflection over the body, to step where the ahead-of-you
already stepped. It is a small skill and a terrible one, because some cold patient
part of you understands that this is how it begins — that you learn to follow the
thing in the water, and then one day the thing in the water leads.
<</linkreplace>>
<</if>>

<<if $fontLooted>>
The coin's gone from the font's lip now — you came back for it — and the figures carved
into the plinth go on kneeling, untroubled by the lifting of what the kneeling drowned
once left.
<<else>>
Coin rings the font's lip, pressed edgewise into the carving by the kneeling dead,
offerings left above the reading-water — there for whoever comes back to gather them.
<</if>>

<<link "Back to the reflecting hall">><<goto "TheReflectingHall">><</link>>
<<link "Along the flooded colonnade">><<goto "TheColonnade">><</link>>


:: TheReliquary [deep]
A drowned vestry, its cupboards burst, their contents settled across the floor
under a hand's depth of still water: a litter of the holy and the looted, chalices
gone green, a reliquary box sprung open on a bone too long to be a finger, votive
coin by the fistful. The water magnifies it all and makes it waver, so that
reaching for a coin you grasp twice before you close on it, as if the water keeps
a copy of everything, and is reluctant to give the copy up.

Wading further in, the room sorts itself out of the murk. The cupboards weren't
burst by violence — their doors hang open the slow way, hinges given up to rot,
nothing knocked, everything simply settled where it sank when the standing-water
came up under it. Vestments float collar-down, their threads unravelling into the
dark in long pale strands. A censer lies tipped on its side, still half-full of
ash that the water hasn't been able to dissolve in all this time, and you find you
have been moving softly, the way you move past a thing that is sleeping rather than
a thing that is dead.

It is the orderliness that gets under you, in the end. Looted vestries are
ransacked; this one was disrobed, gently, by water with nowhere to be, item by item
laid down where it wanted to lie. The bone in the sprung box points off into the
deeper dark like a finger that knows the way, and you understand that nothing was
stolen from here, only *kept* — that this is less a robbed room than a furnished
one, set out and waiting, with a place on the floor still clear for whatever the
flood sees fit to lay down next.

<<if $reliquaryLooted>>
You've taken what spends. The green chalices you leave; nobody up there would buy a
cup that came from down here, and you would not want to explain how you came by it.
<<else>>
Votive coin lies by the fistful under a hand's depth of still water, wavering and
doubled where the water keeps a copy of everything and is reluctant to give the copy
up — there for whoever will put their hands back where they have been.
<</if>>

<<link "Through to the drowned archive">><<goto "TheArchive">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the tomb-island">><<goto "TheTombIsland">><</link>>


:: TheSiltKings [deep]
Twelve thrones, or twelve chairs grand enough to pass for thrones, set in a ring
half-drowned, and in each a seated shape of silt and bone wearing the rotted last
of its finery. The silt-kings. They sat down here in their dignity at some flood or
other and let the water make the rest of the decision, and the water, being
patient, dressed them in itself. Grave-goods glint in their laps and at their feet:
the coin you put on the eyes of the dead, to pay the ferryman, to pay *something*.

<<if $siltKingsLooted>>
The kings keep their thrones. You've taken the toll-coin from their laps; the eyes
you left covered. Some debts you don't want to be the one to call in.
<<else>>
Grave-goods glint in the kings' laps and at their feet — the toll-coin you put on the
eyes of the dead, to pay the ferryman, to pay //something// — there for whoever has the
nerve to come back and lift it from the lap of a king.
<</if>>

<<if not $kingSpoke>>
Eleven of the kings are only silt. The twelfth, when your lamp crosses it, still has something behind
the sockets — a fading dignity that turns its head a fraction, at a cost you can see, and considers you
the way a thing that has watched everything the deep keeps might consider a living guest.
<<linkreplace "Address the one king that still seems to be in there">><<nobr>><<set $kingSpoke to true>>
//Going down to the well, are you,// it says, the voice a settling of silt. //They all do.// The jaw
works. //Mind, then. What's in the well doesn't //cut// — it //gathers.// Put an edge to it and the
edge only parts it, and it closes again behind, patient, and comes on. You don't cut a thing like
that. You //scatter// it. Bring something blunt and heavy — or bring nothing, and if you've nothing,
don't go down.// The dignity settles back into the silt. //That was the last of my breath. Spend
yours better than I spent mine.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The king has settled back into the silt, the brief light behind its sockets gone out; the warning was
the last of its breath, and the ring keeps its long patience without it.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a chamber where a bell hangs">><<goto "TheBellChamber">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the sunken chapel">><<goto "TheSunkenChapel">><</link>>


/* ---------- REST ---------- */

:: TheTombIsland [deep]
Across the black water there is, impossibly, dry land — a raised island of old
tombstone and dressed slab, lifted just clear of the flood, where the dead were
laid before the water rose to take the rest. And these dead are only dead. That is
the whole comfort of the place, and after the drowned galleries it is an enormous
one: properly dead, decently dead, going nowhere, wanting nothing, the first honest
corpses you've met down here.

You sit among them with your back to a slab worn nameless, and the lamp's small
warmth and the simple finished deadness of your company let something in you
unclench. The water laps the island's edge and does not climb it. For a little
while, on the one dry stone in the drowned dark, you are only the living thing in a
room of the safely dead, and that is enough.
<<if $hp lt $maxhp>><<run setup.passTime(setup.REST_TIME)>><</if>><<set $hp to $maxhp>>

<<if not $tombDeadKnown>>
One of the dead is fresher than the rest — laid here before the water quite finished it, and still,
just, able to talk; and when you sit it turns its head, unhurried, glad of the company, and asks
nothing of you but to be heard.
<<linkreplace "Ask the fresh one what kind of place this is">><<nobr>><<set $tombDeadKnown to true>>
//A patient one,// it says. //That's all. People think the deep //kills.// It doesn't kill. Killing's
a thing you do when you're afraid the thing'll get away — and the deep has never once been afraid of
that.// The water laps the island and does not climb it. //It //keeps.// That's the whole of it. The
dirt drains down to feed, and the demons get to //be,// and the rest of us get kept — in the niches,
in the silt, on the dry stone — and being kept //is// the winning, friend. There's no second thing it
wants. You're being kept right now. You just haven't noticed you stopped leaving.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 6>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The fresh one notices what the galleries have started to make of you, and is kind about it, and says
nothing more — there is nothing to say to a thing that is, like everyone here, only being kept.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a sunken chapel">><<goto "TheSunkenChapel">><</link>>
<<link "A flooded vestry, off the slabs">><<goto "TheReliquary">><</link>>
<<link "Back across the causeway to the reflecting hall">><<goto "TheReflectingHall">><</link>>


/* ---------- SNARES (bad ends) ---------- */

:: TheUndertow [deep]
The side-channel looks like all the others — black, still, patient — until you are
beside it and feel the pull: a current where there should be none, a slow steady
draw toward a dark mouth where the water goes down and does not, by any sign, come
back up. Things turn slowly on the surface above the drain of it: a shoe, a pale
splayed hand of root, a circle of bubbles rising in no hurry from something far
below that is still, faintly, letting out its air.

The pull is gentle. That is its whole craft. It does not snatch; it *invites*, the
way a slope invites a tired man to sit, and the water is so smooth and the going-
down would be so easy, and the part of you that is tired — and you are so tired —
leans toward it before the rest of you can vote.

What rises off the dark mouth is not a sound so much as the *idea* of one — the
long sucking note a drain makes far away, felt in the soles of your feet through the
stone, steady as a thing breathing in and never quite breathing back out. You catch
yourself counting the slow turn of the shoe on the surface, the unhurried climb of
the bubbles, the way you'd count something to fall asleep to. The draw has already
taught your weight to lean. All it is waiting on now is for you to agree that the
leaning was your own idea.

<<link "Lean into the pull. Let the water have the weight.">><<goto "EndingUndertow">><</link>>
<<link "Step back from the edge. Hard.">>
  <<soothe 5>>
  <<goto "TheReflectingHall">>
<</link>>


:: TheReflection [deep]
At the colonnade's end the water widens into a still pool, black and broad and
perfectly without ripple, the kind of stillness you have learned down here to read
as a held breath. The last pillars give out into it. There is no far wall you can
find — only the pool, and the dark standing on the pool, and the cold coming off it
in a slow sheet that touches your face like the open door of a cellar.

Then the dark across the pool resolves, and on the far side of it, where there is no
bank for anyone to stand on, *you* are standing — your reflection, but freed of any
surface to be cast on, the half-beat-late you out for a walk of its own. It looks
better than you feel. It is not tired. It is not afraid. It is not filthy. It
regards you across the black water with an expression of enormous, patient sympathy,
and it lifts one clean hand and beckons, and when it speaks the voice is yours from
before all this.

//You've carried it far enough,// it says, kindly. //Let me take a turn at being the
one who's frightened. You can be the one in the water for a while. The water is
restful. Look how rested I am.//

<<linkreplace "Reach across to it — let it take a turn">>
<<corrupt "head" "filth" 32>>
You reach, and it reaches, and your fingers meet at the surface where the cold of
the water is — and something passes the wrong way across that touch. You keep your
body. You keep your place on the dry side. But the tiredness it offered to take, it
took the *opposite* of — it gave you more, pressed a drowned and patient weariness
into you through the fingertips, and stepped back onto its non-existent shore
looking lighter, wearing a little of your warmth now, smiling with what used to be
the muscles of your face.
<</linkreplace>>

<<link "Refuse it. Turn your back on the pool.">>
  <<soothe 8>>
  <<goto "TheColonnade">>
<</link>>


/* ---------- COMBAT ---------- */

:: TheDrownedChoir [deep]
A flooded chancel where the niches do not hold bones but *bodies* — the drowned,
near-whole, kept by the cold black water in the soft grey way water keeps a thing
it isn't finished with. They are arranged facing a drowned altar, kneeling, ranked
like a choir, and as your lamp moves across them their heads turn to follow it, slow
and sodden, water running from the hinges of their jaws.

<<if $drownedChoirCleared>>
The one that rose lies broken on the chancel step, properly drowned at last, and
the rest of the choir kneels on, facing their altar, declining to notice you. You
have the measure of them now: they only rise for the lamp, and only one at a time,
as if there are rules even here.

<<link "Past the choir, to the sunken stair">><<goto "TheSunkenStair">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the ossuary">><<goto "TheCatacomb">><</link>>
<<else>>
One of them stands — unfolds, rather, joint by waterlogged joint — and steps down
off its kneeler into the flood, and comes for the light with its drowned hands out
and its drowned mouth working around water instead of air. The drowned. It does not
hurry. Nothing down here hurries. But it is between you and the deeper dark, and it
means to keep you here, in the cold, in the choir, kneeling.

<<scentof "drowned">>
<<link "Put the drowned thing back down">>
  <<run setup.startFight("drowned", "TheDrownedChoirCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<link "Back to the ossuary, away from the altar">><<goto "TheCatacomb">><</link>>
<</if>>


:: TheDrownedChoirCleared [deep]
/* lands here after the drowned fight; sets the flag, then folds onward */
<<set $drownedChoirCleared to true>>
You put it down the only way that works on the drowned — you break what holds it up,
and let the water have what it was always going to have. It comes apart soft and
grey and grateful, and sinks, and the choir kneels on undisturbed, and past the
altar a stair goes down into a dark even this place is afraid of.

<<link "Down past the altar, to the sunken stair">><<goto "TheSunkenStair">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the ossuary">><<goto "TheCatacomb">><</link>>


/* ---------- DESCENT (foreshadow — the true hell-deep, not yet open) ---------- */

:: TheSunkenStair [deep]
Behind the drowned choir's altar the floor opens on a stair going down — and this
is a different down. The drowned galleries are cold; this is *warm*, a wet animal
warmth that breathes up the stairwell along with a light that is not lamp-light and
not daylight but a low sullen red, like the inside of something. The water that
sheets down these steps is the first warm water you have touched since the sack came
down, and the smell that rises with it is sulphur, and under the sulphur, cooking.

A few treads down, where the red light pools, the stonework changes hands. The
galleries above were *built* — dressed, jointed, laid by people. This is not laid.
The walls of the stairwell are smooth the way a gullet is smooth, run with the same
warm wet that beads on them and slides, and the treads underfoot stop being treads
and start being the suggestion of treads, ridges in something that was never quite
cut by a tool. You have the strong, low, animal sense of having reached the place
where the made world ends and is replaced by the grown one.

This is the way into the true deep — past the mortal sewer entirely, down into the
hell the drowned galleries only drain toward. But the stair is flooded to the arch
a few treads down, a drowned throat of red-lit water with no air at the bottom of
it that you could find, and you are not ready, and some animal certainty tells you
that you will not get a second guess down there. Not yet. Not like this.

<<link "Back up, away from the warm red dark">><<goto "TheDrownedChoir">><</link>>


/* ---------- DEMON BARGAINS (Whisperer / Understudy) ---------- */

:: Cistern [deep]
<<set $metTheWhisperer to true>>
<<nobr>>
The cistern is black glass to the knees, a wide round room walled in slick weeping
brick that climbs past the lamp's reach into nothing. The sound is the first wrong
thing — every drip and every wade of your own feet comes back off the curved walls
doubled, the echo arriving a half-beat behind, so that you seem to be sharing the
room with someone who moves exactly as you do, just late.

Wading toward the centre, you make out the high-water marks: ring on pale ring up
the brick, a hundred floods' worth, the cistern filled and drawn down and filled
again over years you cannot count, and on every ring the same dark tide-line of
whatever the water left when it sank. Something is folded in the far corner — a
smear of wet shadow, two patient lights. The Whisperer. It knows your name, the
private one.
<<if $corruption gte 35>>

//There you are,// it says, almost fond. //I knew you'd come back to me.//
<<else>>

//I know a shortcut up,// it says. //I'll trade you. A small thing. You'll hardly miss it.//
<</if>>
<</nobr>>

<<link "Take its bargain — let it mark you for the way out">>
  <<set $tookTheBargain to true>>
  <<set $foundRealExit to true>>
  <<corrupt "head" "filth" 35>>
  <<goto "DeepHub">>
<</link>>
<<link "Refuse. Wade back out.">>
  <<set $refusedTheWhisperer to true>>
  <<soothe 8>>
  <<goto "DeepHub">>
<</link>>


:: Gallery [deep]
<<set $metUnderstudy to true>>
<<nobr>>
White tile, most of it fallen. What's left is a washroom, or was — a long mirrored
wall above a run of cracked basins, the kind of room built for people to come and
look at themselves in. The flood took the floor and half the tiles with it; the
rest hang on in patches, glazed grey-green, gleaming wetly where the lamp finds
them.

It's the mirror you can't leave alone. In the cracked shards still seated along the
wall, your reflection is a half-step late — and worse, broken across the breaks, so
that each fragment carries a different slice of you doing a slightly different thing,
a face assembled out of mismatched moments, none of them quite now. The Understudy
lives here — a Wearer, the class of demon that covets faces. It has been practising
in the glass for a long time, and as your lamp comes up it is trying on yours,
badly, and smiling with it.
<<if $corruption gte 70>>

//We're nearly the same size now,// it says. //It would barely be a lie.//
<<else>>

//You're so tired of being you,// it says. //Let me carry it a while.//
<</if>>
<</nobr>>

<<link "Let the Understudy take your face for safe passage">>
  <<set $gaveUpFace to true>>
  <<set $foundRealExit to true>>
  <<corrupt "head" "filth" 45>>
  <<goto "DeepHub">>
<</link>>
<<link "Smash the mirror-shards — refuse to be worn">>
  <<soothe 10>>
  <<goto "DeepHub">>
<</link>>


/* ---------- ATMOSPHERE / FILLER ---------- */

:: TheCatacomb [deep]
<<corrupt "torso" "filth" 3>>
The gallery is an ossuary, or it is pretending to be one. Niches line the walls
floor to vault, each holding a bundle of pale shapes stacked the way the
catacomb-makers stacked their dead — long bones crossed, skulls in courses like
masonry. Except the bones are wet, and too many are shaped like nothing that
ever walked, and here and there a niche is swept clean and obviously, horribly,
*waiting*.

You walk the rows the way you'd walk a library, and the longer you read the courses
the less they look stacked by any hand. There is no pattern a mason would own to —
the bones are *settled*, sorted by the slow shuffling of standing water until like
came to lie with like, femur drifted to femur, the small bones pooled low in the
niches like grit in a gutter. Nothing was buried here. Things were simply let go
into the dark, over and over, and the dark filed them where they fell.

You understand, standing here, what kind of place you've fallen into. Not a
sewer. A sewer is what the surface built on top of this, the way a scab builds
over a wound it won't look at. This is the older thing, the thing the filth
drains down to feed. The demons don't haunt the dirt. The dirt is how the demons
get to *be*.

<<link "The tiled gallery, through a low arch">><<goto "Gallery">><</link>>
<<link "A flooded chancel, where a choir kneels">><<goto "TheDrownedChoir">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the plaza of lamps">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>


:: TheColonnade [deep]
A processional walk of paired pillars marching off into the flooded dark, two ranks
of them stepping away from your lamp into a black you can't see the end of. The
water is shin-deep and warm at the edges where it shouldn't be warm at all, and the
pillars go down into it without a base you can find, as if they were always meant to
stand in water and the flood only came to meet them.

It's the capitals that pull your eye up. Each one is carved with a procession of its
own — figures bearing burdens down toward water — and the same scene on every
capital, except not the same: pillar by pillar the burden rides a little heavier and
the bearers stoop a little lower, so that walking the colonnade you are reading a
sentence about going down, written by people who went down, one carved word a pillar.
You find yourself walking slower to keep your place in it, and the deeper you read
the lower the little figures bow, until the last of them are folded nearly double
around a weight the carving never troubles to name.

<<if not $pilgrimHeard>>
A hunched figure paces the pillars, and falls eagerly into step beside you the moment your lamp finds
it, full of the warmth of a man who has been alone with his own story far too long.
<<linkreplace "Hear out the eager pilgrim">><<nobr>><<set $pilgrimHeard to true>>
//A pilgrim, same as yourself — I can tell; you've the walk of a man come down //on purpose.// I gave
up a throne for this road. A wife. Several wives. Walked in past the easy ways, because the hard way's
the only honest one — and these carvings, that bent one's near enough my own likeness; they must have
known I'd come.// He leans in, confiding. //Between two of the devout: I know the dry way out. The real
one, kept for them as walked it right. Come along a little further and I'll put you on it.// The dry
way, you note, is more colonnade; there is always more colonnade. And the capitals over both your heads
show only the burden getting heavier and the bearers bending lower, and not one of them is reaching for
a way out.
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The pilgrim has found someone further down the pillars to be a pilgrim at — you can hear it carrying
off the stone, a different kingdom given up for the road this time. He has stopped meeting your eye;
you both know the dry way out didn't pan out, and neither of you is going to be the one to say so.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a still pool, at the colonnade's end">><<goto "TheReflection">><</link>>
<<link "A font-chamber, off the side">><<goto "TheStillFont">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the plaza of lamps">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>


:: TheBellChamber [deep]
A drowned campanile, fallen on its side and laid out half-submerged across the
chamber floor — a whole bell-tower toppled and kept, its long bricked length running
off under the black water into the dark, and near at hand the great bell itself,
hung sideways now, half its bronze mouth above the surface and half below.

Wading along the fallen tower you can read how it went down: the bell-ropes still
trail from the headstock, swollen and pale, drifting in the slow water like the
roots of something; the louvre-slots that once let the sound out to a sky now let
the flood in instead, dark to dark. When the slow currents move the black water they
move the bell, and it tolls — but the part of its mouth above the water makes no
sound at all, and the part below makes all of it, a deep flooded note you feel in
the wet of your own eyes more than hear.

You stand still and let the note go through you, and the strange thing is how long it
lasts — it goes on well after the bell has settled, soaking up out of the floor and
the walls as if the whole drowned chamber rang and is only now letting the ring back
out. And at the far edge of it, just under the threshold of being sure, the note
seems to be *answered* — the same flooded tone come back fainter from somewhere
further down, from a bell you cannot see, hung in a dark you have not reached yet.

<<link "On toward the silt-kings' ring">><<goto "TheSiltKings">><</link>>
<<link "Through to the drowned archive">><<goto "TheArchive">><</link>>
<<link "Back along the colonnade">><<goto "TheColonnade">><</link>>


:: TheSunkenChapel [deep]
A chapel to nothing the surface would recognise. You come in at the back, where a
nave should be, and the lamp shows it to you in pieces out of the dark: the altar a
slab of the same seeping flowstone as the deep drains, the pews drowned and ranked
and still, the figure above the altar long since dissolved to a suggestion, a lean
wet smear with too many arms folded in patience over a congregation it has clearly
sat with for a very long time.

Going up the flooded aisle, the place keeps its appointments around you. The pews
are barnacled along their tops with a pale freshwater growth, soft and blind, that
has taken the shape of the wood and gone on living past it. Drowned hymn-boards still
carry their numbers, swollen illegible. The kneelers are worn through to the stone in
two pale ovals each, the way only knees wear a thing, by people who knelt and knelt
and meant it. You realise you have lowered your voice, the way you do in a church,
the way you do in a place that is *being used.*

And that is the unbearable part — not that the place is godless, but that it is the
opposite: that it is some god's, and the god kept its appointments, and the drowned
in the niches are not its victims but its *congregation,* sat patient in their rows,
faces to the altar, waiting on a service that down here has plainly never been called
off.

<<if not $communicantHeard>>
A wet shape kneels in the back pew, head bowed to the dissolved figure above the altar, and does not
break off its devotions when you enter — but it speaks, when you draw near, in the easy voice of
someone who has made his peace and would like to share it.
<<linkreplace "A kneeler in the back pew lifts its head — speak to it">><<nobr>><<set $communicantHeard to true>>
//They told it wrong, up top — that this place //takes// you. It doesn't take. Taking would mean it
wanted you somewhere you weren't.// It does not turn its head. //No. You walk in on your own two feet,
and you find it warm, and you find it kept, and you kneel — because your knees are tired and the god's
so patient — and that's the whole of it. Nobody drowned me, friend. I sat down. The water only saw to
the rest, the way water does: it finds the lowest part of you, and it fills it.// A pause, almost
gentle. //Sit. You'll find you already want to.//
<</nobr>><<corrupt "head" "filth" 4>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The communicant has gone back to facing its god, lips moving on a service with no word in it but
//stay;// it said the only thing it had, and it was an invitation, and you are still standing, which
is its own kind of answer.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward an island of dry tombs">><<goto "TheTombIsland">><</link>>
<<link "A ring of half-drowned thrones">><<goto "TheSiltKings">><</link>>
<<link "A black well, sunk in the chapel floor">><<goto "TheDeepWell">><</link>>


:: TheArchive [deep]
A drowned archive — racks of it marching back into the dark, rank on rank, more than
your lamp can hold at once. Ledgers and rolls and pigeonholed scrolls, all of it
swollen to grey pulp, all of it shelved still in the order someone shelved it, a
whole library standing patient under water.

The water itself, you realise as you wade in, is the archive now. The ink has long
since bled out of every page and into the flood, so that the room is faintly,
permanently clouded — a held suspension of everything that was ever written here,
dissolved and turning slowly in the lamp-light, drifting between the racks in pale
threads you could almost mistake for words if you let your eyes go soft. You breathe
it. You wade through it. Whatever was set down in this place is in the water now, and
the water is in you.

The Whisperer's trade, you think, was kept somewhere like this once: all the small
private names, all the things people would rather not, written down by something
patient and filed against the day it would be useful to know them — and not lost when
the flood came, only loosed, only made into the medium itself, so that the deep need
never look a thing up again. It simply knows. It has been drinking its own ledgers
for years.

<<if not $scrivenerTold>>
A waterlogged shape is still seated at a rack, working through the dissolved ledgers with sodden,
patient fingers — the filing-hand that wrote the names down before ever the Whisperer traded them. It
does not threaten. It offers, instead, to read you the one entry still legible.
<<linkreplace "Let it read you the one legible entry">><<nobr>><<set $scrivenerTold to true>><<if not $heardOfWord>><<set $heardOfWord to true>><</if>>
It draws a swollen finger down a page that is mostly water, and what it reads you is not a name but a
fact: that the deep //writes.// That all of this — the ledgers, the names cut over the gates, the close
wet script the dark keeps — was //written,// and a thing that was written can be //read,// by anyone
patient enough to stop being frightened of it. //The great arch you came down through,// it says, //the
jaw of fused bone, all those names over the throat — that's no tally, child. That's a primer. Go back
and stand under it like you mean to read it, and it will read back.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
It has gone back to its filing; the ink it read you has already bled back into the water, and the rack
keeps its patience, filing against a day that has, for it, already come.
<</if>>

<<link "Through to the flooded vestry">><<goto "TheReliquary">><</link>>
<<link "Toward a weeping arch">><<goto "TheWeepingArch">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the bell-chamber">><<goto "TheBellChamber">><</link>>


:: TheWeepingArch [deep]
An arch of black stone spans the passage ahead, and the wall around it is wet — not
seeping, not damp, but *running,* a whole face of stone in motion under the lamp,
sheeted top to bottom with a film of water that should have no business being up
there. There is no far side that should hold water higher than this. And yet the arch
weeps, a steady sheet running down off its keystone and pooling at your feet to join
the flood you stand in.

Step under it and look up, and the carving resolves out of the wet: the arch's face
is a *face,* vast and worn near to smooth, brow and lidded eyes and a long patient
mouth, the whole drowned galleries running out of its eyes a drop at a time. The
water beads in the carved sockets and spills and tracks down the cheeks in the exact
worn channels a thousand years of weeping have cut, so that you cannot tell whether
the mason carved a weeping face or the weeping wore the face into the stone.

You could believe, standing in the drip of it, that the entire deep is this one slow
grief — that the water you have waded through all this long way is the same water,
going round and round: wept out of these eyes, drained back down through the
galleries, lifted somehow, and wept out again. The floods, the kept dead, you in the
middle of it — all of it just the inside of a single enormous sorrow, crying quietly
to itself in the dark since long before there were people to drown in it.

<<if not $sluicemanHeard>>
A man in a wet leather apron sits under the arch with a bucket between his boots, dipping it and tipping
it back into the flood to no result he seems to expect or want — a works hand, by the look, on a shift
that has plainly not ended in a very long while.
<<linkreplace "Have a word with the man and his bucket">><<nobr>><<set $sluicemanHeard to true>>
//Don't gawp at the face, it's only a head-tank weeping its overflow — every system's got to weep
somewhere.// He tips the pail without looking. //Same water the whole way down, mind; I've watched it
go round long enough to know my own bucketful by sight. I came down on a relief shift — double-time,
they said, six weeks below the old drains and back up by the saint's day. That were the bargain.
Nobody mentioned the //up-by// part again, and after a while you stop bringing it up yourself, on
account of it sounds like complaining and there's no one down here to complain to that isn't also on
shift.// He works his shoulder, the way a man does at the end of a long one. //You're not the relief,
are you. No. They never send the relief.//
<</nobr>><</linkreplace>>
<<else>>
The sluiceman has the bucket going again, fill and tip and fill, and only nods when you come back — a
man on shift to a man passing through, with no more to say, because the saying isn't in the job and the
job is all that's left of him.
<</if>>

<<link "Toward a black well in the chapel floor">><<goto "TheDeepWell">><</link>>
<<link "Back to the drowned archive">><<goto "TheArchive">><</link>>


:: TheDeepWell [deep]
A well, sunk in the chapel floor, ringed with the same worn flowstone and brimming
to its lip with water blacker than any you have come through yet — so black it does
not read as water at all but as a hole cut clean through the world, a round of pure
absence the lamp will not light and your eye keeps sliding off.

You crouch at the rim and sound it the old way. You drop a coin and wait for the
strike and never hear it — not a splash, not a click, nothing, the coin simply gone
into the down. You drop a second and lean to listen, and that is the mistake, because
the listening *pulls,* a slow steady draw up out of the throat of the thing, and you
understand all at once that a well like this is only an undertow stood on its end, and
the down of it is very patient and very deep and has, plainly, all the time there is.

The drowned galleries drain to here. You can feel it now that you're over it — the
whole flooded floor of the deep tilting some imperceptible degree toward this one
round mouth, the colonnade and the chapel and the bell-tower all leaning their water
slowly this way. Everything drains to here. And eventually, the certainty rises in
you cold and sure as the cold off the water, so will you.

<<if $glutFelled>>
The well is only a well now. Whatever had gathered itself in the dark at the bottom
of it is scattered through the water, parted and sinking and unmaking, and the black
surface lies flat and stupid and drains, at last, like plain water drains. You can
lean over it and hear, very far down, the small ordinary sound of a coin striking
stone.
<<else>>
And something, far down, is listening back. You feel it the way you feel a held
breath in a dark room — a vast slow attention gathering itself out of the settled
filth of a thousand drained years, the muck of everything that ever washed down to
here remembering, all at once, that it is more than muck. The water domes. It is
going to come up whether you call it or not; the only choice the well leaves you is
whether to be leaning over it when it does. Already something smaller is over the lip
ahead of it — a lesser golem, shed off the great one's skirts as it rises, the way a
wave throws spray before the weight of it arrives. Nothing that size comes up alone.

<<scentof `["glut","shitgolem"]`>>
<<link "Wake what's down there — meet it on your feet">>
  <<run setup.startFight(["glut", "shitgolem"], "TheDeepWellCleared")>>
  <<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<</if>>

<<link "Step back from the well">><<goto "TheSunkenChapel">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the weeping arch">><<goto "TheWeepingArch">><</link>>


:: TheDeepWellCleared [deep]
/* lands here after the Glut fight (dynamic — reached via $combatReturn, not a static goto;
   allowed orphan, like the other *Cleared landers). Sets the flag, hands the reward: the coin
   it swallowed out of everyone who ever drained here, and — coughed up from the middle of it —
   the golem-breaker maul, the one tool ever made to kill its kind, lost down here and recovered
   from inside the thing it was forged against. First and only source of that weapon. */
<<set $glutFelled to true>>
<<nobr>>
You do it the only way the muck allows — not with the edge, which only ever parts it and lets it
close again, but by //scattering// it: blow on heavy blow until the will holding it together has
nowhere left to gather, and the Glut comes apart the way a wave comes apart on a breakwater, all
at once and everywhere, and is only water again. The lesser thing it shed went the same way
first — spray, before the wave.
<</nobr>>

<<set _c to random(28, 44)>>
What it leaves behind settles out of the parting filth onto the well's lip: ''<<= _c>> coins'',
cold and uncountable, the toll of everyone the drains ever took — and, heavier, half-swallowed and
streaked with the thing's own slime, a long iron head you know on sight. A maul. //The// maul:
the golem-breaker, lost down here in some earlier life by some earlier body, gone down the same
black throat as the coin and the dead and never digested, because the one thing the filth could
never make a part of itself was the tool built to scatter it.
<<set _got to setup.gainCoins(_c)>>
<<if _got lte 0>>You gather the cold coin off the lip and it curdles to rubbish in your fist, the curse on you eating every piece — but the maul, at least, is iron the curse cannot touch.<</if>>
<<run setup.acquire("golembreaker")>>

<<link "Take the maul and step back from the well">><<goto "TheSunkenChapel">><</link>>
<<link "Toward the weeping arch">><<goto "TheWeepingArch">><</link>>


/* ---------- CLEAN-VS-DIRTY FORK: the sluice (waders) — owner 2026-06-07 ----------
   Two ways to the dry relief-vault: the GUARDED maintenance catwalk (fight the drowned OR pay it
   off — clean either way) or the DISCREET wade through the flooded sluice (clean if you have waders,
   else the black water gets into your legs). New optional pocket; loops back to the plaza. */

:: TheSluiceFork [deep]
<<nobr>>
A relief-sluice opens off the plaza, a low brick throat where the standing water gives way at last to
water that *moves* — a slow black current drawing off into the dark, the first thing down here that has
anywhere it would rather be. Somewhere past it the niche-marks promise a maintenance vault the flood
never reached, with dry stores still on its shelves.
<</nobr>>

<<nobr>>
There are two ways to it, and the sluice lays them out for you plain. A narrow iron catwalk runs above
the water, dry and sound and railed for working men — but a drowned man stands on it, hip-deep where it
dips, turned to face you, and does not stand aside; he has the patience of a thing with nowhere else it
needs to be and all the time not to be there. And below the catwalk the sluice itself runs full and
black and unhurried, deep enough to wade, and you could simply go that way, down into the moving dark
and past him entirely — if you fancy what wading a current this old would put into you.
<</nobr>>

<<if $sumpVaultLooted>>
You have already been through to the vault; the way is known now, the drowned gone back to its
waiting, the water as it was.
<<link "Through to the relief-vault">><<goto "TheSumpVault">><</link>>
<<else>>
//You can read the price of each way from where you stand: the drowned wants a real fight, or ''12 coins'' to stand aside; and the sluice would put ''about 16 filth'' into your legs<<if setup.has("waders")>> — but your waders turn that aside<</if>>.//

<<scentof "drowned">>
<<link "The guarded way — put the drowned man down and take the catwalk">>
  <<run setup.startFight("drowned", "TheSumpVault")>><<goto "Combat">>
<</link>>
<<if $coins gte 12>>
<<link "The guarded way — pay the drowned its toll (12 coins), pass dry">>
  <<set $coins to $coins - 12>><<goto "TheSumpVault">>
<</link>>
<<else>>(The drowned will let you by for 12 coins; you are short.)<</if>>
<<if setup.has("waders")>>
<<link "The discreet way — wade the sluice (your waders keep it off you)">>
  <<goto "TheSumpVault">>
<</link>>
<<else>>
<<set _tlbl to "The discreet way — wade the black sluice (" + setup.tollTag("legL", "filth", 16) + ")">><<link _tlbl>>
  <<corrupt "legL" "filth" 16>>
  <<goto "TheSumpVault">>
<</link>>
<</if>>
<</if>>
<<link "Back to the plaza of lamps">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>


:: TheSumpVault [deep]
<<nobr>>
The vault is exactly the small mercy the marks promised: a dry stone cell above the waterline, its
shelves still holding what someone stored against a flood that came anyway and never left. Cold,
quiet, and — for the deep — almost safe.
<</nobr>>

<<nobr>>
It takes a moment to credit the dryness. After so long with the black water in your boots the floor
here is just stone, plain dry stone, and the sound has gone with the water — no drip, no lap, only your
own breath off the close walls. A stub of tallow sits hardened in a dish by the door, a folded blanket
gone stiff on a low cot, the leavings of a works-hand who meant to wait the flood out in here and, by
the orderly look of the place, simply never had the chance to leave.
<</nobr>>

<<if not $sumpVaultLooted>>
<<set $sumpVaultLooted to true>>
<<set _g to setup.gainCoins(random(8, 14))>>
You sweep <<= _g>> cold coin<<if _g neq 1>>s<</if>> from a tin, and a wad of grave-moss salve from a
shelf, into your pack.
<<run $inventory.push("salve")>>
<<else>>
The shelves are bare now; you have already had what they held.
<</if>>

<<link "Back out, to the plaza">><<goto "DeepHub">><</link>>


/* ---------- LAYER-4 BAD END ---------- */

:: EndingUndertow [ending]
<<run setup.recordRun("died")>>
You let the weight go, and the water takes it, and the going-down is exactly as
restful as it promised — no snatch, no struggle, just the smooth cold sliding-under
of a tired thing finally allowed to stop holding itself up. The last of the lamp-
light wavers and shrinks to a coin of grey far above, the way the daylight did at
the top of the shaft you never reached, and then the patient dark closes over it,
and over you, without a ripple to mark that anything was ever there to drown.

The drowned galleries keep what the water hands them. Somewhere far below, in no
hurry at all, something lets out a slow breath of bubbles, and makes room.

//You went under looking for an easier way down. The water was glad to help.//

<<link "Again">><<run setup.restartCarry()>><</link>>
